---
/**
 * A pull quote: one important line from the post, set big in a yellow card so a skimming reader
 * stops on it. Put it right after the paragraph that already contains the line, and use the exact
 * same words.
 *
 * Why it looks like this: section headings on this site are Fraunces serif on the page color with a
 * thin rule above. A pull quote must never be mistaken for one, so it shares almost nothing with
 * them: a solid yellow fill, bold sans (Space Grotesk, not the serif), a black quote chip, a thick
 * border with a hard shadow, a slight tilt, and from 72rem it sticks out past the text column.
 *
 * Why <aside aria-hidden="true">: the line is already in the paragraph above, so a screen reader
 * would read it twice. It is a repeat of body text, not a quotation of someone else, so it is not a
 * <blockquote>. Hiding it from assistive tech loses nothing. Sighted readers see it, and it holds
 * no links or buttons, so nothing focusable gets hidden. The quote mark is CSS only.
 *
 * Usage in MDX: <PullQuote>Exact line from the post.</PullQuote>
 */
---

<aside class="pq" aria-hidden="true">
  <p class="pq-text"><slot /></p>
</aside>

<style>
  .pq {
    position: relative;
    /* Room above for the quote chip, and below for the hard shadow. */
    margin: var(--spacing-lg) 0 var(--spacing-lg);
    padding: var(--spacing-md) var(--spacing-sm) var(--spacing-md);
    /* The card is yellow in light and dark, so its text and border are fixed, not theme colors. */
    background: var(--color-yellow);
    color: var(--color-black);
    border: var(--rule-thick) solid var(--edge);
    box-shadow: 8px 8px 0 var(--edge);
    transform: rotate(-1deg);
  }
  /* The quote chip: a black square that sits on the top-left corner, holding a yellow quote mark.
     The mark is generated text with an empty alternative, so it is never read aloud. */
  .pq::before {
    content: "\201C";
    content: "\201C" / "";
    position: absolute;
    top: -1.5rem;
    left: var(--spacing-sm);
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    width: 3rem;
    height: 3rem;
    background: var(--color-black);
    color: var(--color-yellow);
    border: var(--rule-thick) solid var(--edge);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 900;
    font-size: 3.5rem;
    /* The curly quote sits high in its box; the line height and padding pull it to the middle. */
    line-height: 1;
    padding-top: 0.75rem;
    box-sizing: border-box;
  }
  .pq-text {
    margin: 0;
    padding-top: var(--spacing-sm);
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: var(--text-xl);
    line-height: 1.2;
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-tight);
    text-wrap: balance;
  }
  @media (min-width: 48rem) {
    .pq {
      padding: var(--spacing-md);
    }
    .pq::before {
      left: var(--spacing-md);
    }
  }
  /* Where the column has room (checked at 72rem: column 43rem, text 40rem), the card sticks out
     to the right of the text column by 3rem. */
  @media (min-width: 72rem) {
    .pq {
      margin-inline-end: calc(var(--spacing-lg) * -1);
    }
  }
  /* A tilted card is only for people who are fine with it; flat is calmer. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .pq {
      transform: none;
    }
  }
</style>
